{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>The crown of the elderly is grandchildren, and the glory of children is their fathers (Proverbs 17:6).</b>" "<b>Solomon,</b> of blessed memory, informs us in this verse (Proverbs 17), according to its plain sense, that children are a crown and glory to fathers — for we find with Abraham that his father Terah was crowned with him; this is 'the crown of the elderly is grandchildren.' But fathers are a glory to children, for when the fathers are righteous and the children glory in their fathers, this is to them for honor and glory. Therefore it says 'glory' and did not say 'crown,' since a crown is greater than glory according to the plain sense, and a greater praise. And when are the fathers a glory to children? When the children do the deeds of their fathers — then they are a glory to them. But if they do not hold fast to the deeds of their fathers, the matter is not for glory but for shame and even for reproach and great deficiency, like a branch that denies its root." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> 'The crown of the elderly is grandchildren' — the righteous are crowned with their children. This is Abraham, who was crowned with the merit of Jacob his grandson when Nimrod cast him into the fiery furnace and the Holy One, blessed be He, saved him in the merit of Jacob his grandson. This is what is written (Isaiah 29:22): 'Therefore thus says YHWH concerning the house of Jacob, who redeemed Abraham' — He redeemed him from the furnace. This is the fathers who were crowned with their children. The children are crowned with their fathers: when Jacob went out from Laban, he pursued him; the Holy One, blessed be He, revealed Himself to him on the way and said, 'Take heed from speaking with Jacob,' etc. When they came to contend on the mountain, he (Laban) said to him (Genesis 31:42), 'Were it not for the Elohim of Abraham and the Dread of Isaac had been for me' — see how the children were crowned with the merit of the fathers, for the wicked Laban's intention to do evil to Jacob was not fulfilled. And so, in the merit of the fathers, the decree of Pharaoh was not fulfilled — he decreed 'every son that is born, cast into the river,' etc. — and it was turned back upon him (Exodus 15:4): 'Pharaoh's chariots and his army He cast into the sea.' And so, in the merit of the fathers, the intention of Haman was not fulfilled — he sought to destroy, slay, and annihilate all the Jews, and the Holy One, blessed be He, overturned his counsel, and it was turned upon him, as is said (Esther 9): 'and it was turned about.' And so Balaam sought to curse all Israel, and it was turned upon him, as is written: 'and YHWH your Elohim turned the curse into a blessing for you.' And this is what the prophet said (Isaiah 54:17): 'Every weapon formed against you shall not prosper.' And so our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: the Emperor Hadrian said to Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi, 'Great is the power of a sheep that stands among seventy wolves.' He said to him, 'Great is the shepherd who rescues her from their hand and breaks them before her,' as is said: 'every weapon formed against you,' etc. And the explanation of the matter concerns the idolaters who shall perish from the world, in the manner of what is written (Jeremiah 2:3): 'Israel is holy to YHWH, the first of His increase; all who consume him shall be held guilty; evil shall come upon them, says YHWH.' And so we find with all those who came to do evil to Israel, that they did not succeed and perished in the end — such as Pharaoh, and Amalek, and Sisera, and Sennacherib, and Nebuchadnezzar, and Titus, and Hadrian, and those like them. Therefore He compared Israel to קדש (terumah), for just as terumah is called קדש, and if a stranger eats of it he is liable to death, so the strangers who consume Israel are destined to be destroyed from the world — this is 'all who consume him shall be held guilty.' And so we find with Abraham, that he was crowned with Isaac his son, and Isaac was crowned with Abraham his father, because the scorners of the generation would say, 'Sarah conceived from Abimelech, and Isaac is the son of Abimelech'; therefore the Torah needed to testify that Abraham begot Isaac. And this is what it says:" "<b>And these are the generations of Isaac, son of Abraham: Abraham begot Isaac.</b> Our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: just as you find with Abraham and Isaac, so you find with David and Chileab. For when David's messengers went to Nabal, Nabal answered (1 Samuel 25:10): 'Who is David, and who is the son of Jesse? Nowadays there are many servants who break loose,' etc., 'and shall I take my bread, and my water, and my meat that I have prepared for my shearers, and give it to men whom I do not know where they are from?' Immediately David armed himself and his men to go up against them. What did Abigail do? She prepared a gift, as is written: 'And Abigail hurried and took two hundred loaves of bread, and said to her young men, Go on before me.' And it is written: 'And it came to pass about ten days later, and HaShem struck Nabal, and he died.' Immediately: 'And David sent and spoke with Abigail, to take her for himself as a wife.' When she came, immediately David separated from her for three months. After three months he came to her and she conceived from him, and the scorners of the generation would say, 'She is pregnant from Nabal.' What did the Holy One, blessed be He, do? He shaped the features of his face like the likeness of David. This is what is written: 'and his second, Chileab, of Abigail the wife of Nabal the Carmelite.' What is 'Chileab' (כלאב)? כולו אב — 'entirely father.'"], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:15:16.302396870Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}